<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="topic-3-3-libraries">
  <time minutes="90" />
  <title>Using Existing Code and Libraries</title>

  <idx>libraries</idx>
  <introduction>
     <p>Quick Links: 
      <xref text="global" ref="libraries, apis, LOs-3-3, vocab-3-1"/>
    </p>
    <p>In this unit, we will learn how to use existing code and libraries.
    </p>
       
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="libraries">
    <title>Libraries</title>
  
    <note>
      <title>3.3.A.1</title>
      <p>
        A software <term>library</term> is a collection of prewritten 
        procedures that programmers can use to add features to a 
        program without writing everything from scratch (for 
        example, drawing graphics, working with data, or 
        generating random values).
      </p>
    </note>

      <note>
      <title>3.3.A.2</title>
      <p>
       Existing code segments can come from internal sources 
       (your own prior projects or your team’s code) or external 
       sources (libraries, sample code, or online repositories). 
       Reusing code can save time and reduce errors when the 
       code is well-tested and well-understood.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.3.A.3</title>
      <p>
        Libraries and reusable code simplify complex tasks by 
        providing procedures that handle common problems, 
        allowing programmers to focus on the parts of the 
        program that are unique to their users and goals.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="apis">
    <title>Application Programming Interfaces (APIs)</title>
    
    <note>
      <title>3.3.A.4</title>
      <p>
        An <term>application programming interface (API)</term> 
        is a set of rules that specifies how one piece of 
        software can interact with another. An API describes 
        what inputs are accepted, what output is returned (if 
        any), and what side effects may occur.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.3.A.5</title>
      <p>
        Library documentation describes the API and is necessary 
        for correct use. Programmers use documentation to learn 
        required inputs, possible outputs, examples, limitations, 
        and common errors.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.3.A.6</title>
      <p>
        Selecting appropriate libraries or code segments requires 
        checking whether they fit the program’s purpose and user 
        needs, are compatible with the programming environment, 
        come from a trustworthy source, and are licensed for reuse.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.3.A.7</title>
      <p>
        When using existing code, programmers test how reused 
        procedures work within their own program. They compare 
        actual results to expected results using both typical 
        inputs and boundary cases to confirm the integration 
        works as intended.
      </p>
    </note>

    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="LOs-3-3">
    <title>Learning Objectives Review</title>

    <remark>
      <title>3.3.A</title>
      <p>
        Develop code using appropriate libraries or existing code segments to create a program.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="vocab-3-3">
   <title>Vocabulary Review</title>
  </subsection>  

</section>